sysl.regex — the lazy DFA
=========================

    module sysl.regex

    import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
    import sysl.text.{Chars, is_char_boundary}

The Pike VM in `vm` keeps a list of threads and walks every one of them at every character. That is
what bounds it, and it is also what makes it slow: a search of a long text spends nearly all its time
rebuilding the same few lists over and over, because a pattern has only so many ways of being part
way through. This file remembers them.

A **state** here is the set of instructions the VM's list would hold at some position, and the
**transition** from one state on a character is the list the VM would build for the next position.
Both are worked out the first time they are needed and kept, so the second time a search is in that
state and reads that character, the step is one lookup in a table. That is Thompson's construction
again, determinised lazily — only the states a text actually reaches are ever built, which is what
keeps a pattern whose full DFA would be exponential from costing more than the VM it replaces.

The VM is still the engine of record. This answers only **where** the leftmost-longest match is, and
whenever a pattern has capture groups the VM is run again over exactly that span to fill them in.


What a state has to remember
----------------------------

The VM's list is ordered, and the order is what decides the answer: threads started earlier sit in
front of threads started later, so an earlier start claims an instruction first. A state keeps that
much of the order and no more — its instructions are kept in **groups, one per starting position**,
earliest first, and each group is a sorted set. Order *within* a group decides only which thread
carries which capture positions, and this engine reports none.

That the groups may be sets rests on one fact: an instruction at a position that some earlier start
also reaches can never lead to the leftmost match, because if it led to an accept, the earlier start
would have matched first. So the VM dropping the later thread there loses nothing, and neither does
dropping the instruction from the later group.

A group is laid down as its instructions followed by `-1`, and a state is its groups end to end.

Instructions that look at the input land in a state, and so does `accept`. The assertions are
decided by the position, and the position is not part of a state — the same state is reached at
many positions — so one of them is decided by where the step happens and the other is **kept
pending**. Searching forward, `^` can only hold where the search began, so it is settled there; `$`
holds only at the end of the text and stays in the state until the end is reached. Searching
backward the two swap roles.

Three facts about a state are read on every step, so each is a bit of one word:

    private const has_accept: int = 1
    private const is_dead: int = 2
    private const no_starts: int = 4

`has_accept` — some group holds `accept`, so a match ends here. `no_starts` — something has already
matched, so no later position may begin one (the VM's own rule: nothing that begins later can win).
`is_dead` — no group is left and none can begin, so the search is over.

A table row has a slot for every ASCII byte. A character outside ASCII is stepped without the table,
which is slower and exactly as correct; text that is mostly ASCII is what a table is worth building
for.

    private const row: usize = 128

The cache is bounded by states and by the instructions they hold together. Reaching either bound
throws every state away and carries on from the one the search is in, so a pattern whose reachable
states outnumber the cache costs more per character but never more memory.

    private const cache_states: usize = 2048
    private const cache_pool: usize = 1048576

    private[regex] struct Dfa
        prog: Program

        // Searching toward the start of the text: the program is the pattern reversed, it is
        // anchored where the search begins, and `^` is the assertion left pending.
        backward: bool

        pool: &Buf[int]
        first: &Buf[int]
        count: &Buf[int]
        flags: &Buf[int]
        trans: &Buf[int]
        table: &Buf[int]
        starts: &Buf[int]
        marks: &Buf[int]
        work: &Buf[int]
        gen: int
        flushes: int

The closure, as the VM's `add` computes it, written into `work`. The marks are the VM's marks — one
stamp per instruction, compared against the step's own — and they are shared by every group built
in one step, which is what lets an earlier group claim an instruction before a later one can.

        private follow(*self, pc: usize, at_start: bool, at_end: bool)
            if pc >= self.prog.len() || self.marks[pc] == self.gen then return

            self.marks[pc] = self.gen

            self.prog.at(pc) match
                Jump(to) -> self.follow(to, at_start, at_end)
                Split(a, b) ->
                    self.follow(a, at_start, at_end)
                    self.follow(b, at_start, at_end)
                Save(_) -> self.follow(pc + 1, at_start, at_end)
                AssertStart ->
                    if at_start then
                        self.follow(pc + 1, at_start, at_end)
                    elif self.backward then
                        self.work.push(int(pc))
                AssertEnd ->
                    if at_end then
                        self.follow(pc + 1, at_start, at_end)
                    elif !self.backward then
                        self.work.push(int(pc))
                else self.work.push(int(pc))
        end follow

A group is closed by sorting what was added since `lo` and writing the separator, so two groups
holding the same instructions in different orders are one state. An empty group is not written at
all: a start that has nothing left alive is not a start any more.

        private close_group(*self, lo: usize)
            val hi = self.work.len()

            if hi == lo then return

            for i in (lo + 1)..<hi
                val v = self.work[i]
                var j = i

                while j > lo && self.work[j - 1] > v
                    self.work[j] = self.work[j - 1]
                    j -= 1

                self.work[j] = v

            self.work.push(-1)
        end close_group

Finding a state again. `work` holds the candidate; an open-addressed table of state numbers, keyed
by a hash of the groups and the `no_starts` bit, answers whether it was built before.

        private same(self, s: usize, starts_done: bool) -> bool
            if ((self.flags[s] & no_starts) != 0) != starts_done then return false

            val n = usize(self.count[s])

            if n != self.work.len() then return false

            val at = usize(self.first[s])

            for i in 0..<n
                if self.pool[at + i] != self.work[i] then return false

            true
        end same

        private intern(*self, starts_done: bool) -> int
            var h: u64 = if starts_done then 7 else 3

            for i in 0..<self.work.len()
                h = (h ^ u64.wrap(self.work[i])) *% 1099511628211

            val mask = self.table.len() - 1
            var slot = usize(h & u64(mask))

            loop
                val e = self.table[slot]

                if e == 0 then break

                if self.same(usize(e - 1), starts_done) then return e - 1

                slot = (slot + 1) & mask

            val full = self.first.len() >= cache_states || self.pool.len() + self.work.len() > cache_pool

            if full && self.first.len() > 0
                self.flush()
                return self.intern(starts_done)

            val id = int(self.first.len())
            var f = if starts_done then no_starts else 0

            for i in 0..<self.work.len()
                val pc = self.work[i]

                if pc >= 0 && is_accept(self.prog.at(usize(pc))) then f |= has_accept

            if self.work.len() == 0 && (starts_done || self.backward) then f |= is_dead

            self.first.push(int(self.pool.len()))
            self.count.push(int(self.work.len()))
            self.pool.extend(self.work.view())
            self.flags.push(f)

            for _ in 0..<row
                self.trans.push(-1)

            self.table[slot] = id + 1
            id
        end intern

        private flush(*self)
            self.pool.clear()
            self.first.clear()
            self.count.clear()
            self.flags.clear()
            self.trans.clear()

            for i in 0..<self.table.len()
                self.table[i] = 0

            for i in 0..<self.starts.len()
                self.starts[i] = -1

            self.flushes += 1
        end flush

The state a search begins in. Where it begins is known exactly, so both assertions are decided
there rather than left pending, and the four ways that can come out are each kept.

        private start(*self, at_start: bool, at_end: bool) -> int
            val k = (if at_start then 2 else 0) + (if at_end then 1 else 0)

            if self.starts[k] >= 0 then return self.starts[k]

            self.gen += 1
            self.work.clear()
            self.follow(0, at_start, at_end)
            self.close_group(0)

            val s = self.intern(false)

            self.starts[k] = s
            s
        end start

One step: each group in order moves over the character, and a new group begins behind them unless
something has matched. A group holding `accept` is the leftmost match so far, so **every group after
it is dropped** — they began later and cannot win — while the group itself keeps going, since a
longer match from the same start beats the one just found. That is the VM's "leftmost, then longest"
in terms of groups.

        private step(*self, s: usize, c: char) -> int
            self.gen += 1
            self.work.clear()

            var i = usize(self.first[s])
            val stop = i + usize(self.count[s])
            var accepted = false

            while i < stop && !accepted
                val lo = self.work.len()

                while self.pool[i] >= 0
                    val pc = usize(self.pool[i])

                    self.prog.at(pc) match
                        MatchChar(want) -> if c == want then self.follow(pc + 1, false, false)
                        MatchAny -> self.follow(pc + 1, false, false)
                        MatchSet(cls) -> if cls.admits(c) then self.follow(pc + 1, false, false)
                        Accept -> accepted = true
                        else ()

                    i += 1

                i += 1
                self.close_group(lo)

            val starts_done = (self.flags[s] & no_starts) != 0 || accepted

            if !starts_done && !self.backward
                val lo = self.work.len()

                self.follow(0, false, false)
                self.close_group(lo)

            self.intern(starts_done)
        end step

The step through the table, building the entry the first time. A flush in the middle of building
it leaves `s` naming nothing, so the entry is only written when the cache survived.

An entry is written so that the inner loops below need one load and one comparison per byte. A
target state with nothing remarkable about it is stored as **where its own row begins** (`t * row`),
so the loop goes on from there without a multiplication; one that accepts or is dead is stored as
`-2 - t`, and an entry not yet built as `-1`. Any negative number sends the loop out to here.

        private next(*self, s: int, b: u8) -> int
            val slot = usize(s) * row + usize(b)
            val t = self.trans[slot]

            if t >= 0 then return t / int(row)

            if t < -1 then return -2 - t

            val before = self.flushes
            val built = self.step(usize(s), char(u32(b)))

            if self.flushes == before
                val plain = (self.flags[usize(built)] & (has_accept | is_dead)) == 0

                self.trans[slot] = if plain then built * int(row) else -2 - built

            built
        end next

Whether a match ends at the last position the search reaches, where the pending assertion is
finally decided. It is asked once per search, so it is worked out rather than kept.

        private ends_here(*self, s: int, at_start: bool, at_end: bool) -> bool
            if (self.flags[usize(s)] & has_accept) != 0 then return true

            self.gen += 1
            self.work.clear()

            val lo = usize(self.first[usize(s)])

            for i in lo..<(lo + usize(self.count[usize(s)]))
                val pc = self.pool[i]

                if pc >= 0
                    self.prog.at(usize(pc)) match
                        AssertStart -> self.follow(usize(pc) + 1, at_start, at_end)
                        AssertEnd -> self.follow(usize(pc) + 1, at_start, at_end)
                        else ()

            for i in 0..<self.work.len()
                if is_accept(self.prog.at(usize(self.work[i]))) then return true

            false
        end ends_here


Forward: where the match ends
-----------------------------

The search runs until no group is left alive, remembering the last position a match ended at. That
is the end of the leftmost-longest match: once a group matches, every later one is gone, so a match
seen afterwards comes either from that same group, ending later, or from an earlier one, beginning
sooner — and either beats what was remembered.

`first_only` stops at the first match instead, which is all `is_match` needs to know.

The inner loop is the reason for all of the above: while the table already knows the way and the
state is unremarkable, a character costs one load from the table and one comparison.

        private[regex] find_end(*self, src: []const u8, from: usize, first_only: bool) -> int
            val n = src.len
            var s = self.start(from == 0, from == n)
            var i = from
            var last = -1

            loop
                val f = self.flags[usize(s)]

                if (f & has_accept) != 0
                    last = int(i)

                    if first_only then return last

                if (f & is_dead) != 0 then return last

                if i >= n
                    if self.ends_here(s, n == 0, true) then last = int(n)

                    return last

                val b = src[i]

                if b < 128
                    s = self.next(s, b)
                    i += 1

                    if (self.flags[usize(s)] & (has_accept | is_dead)) == 0
                        val tr = self.trans.view()
                        var o = usize(s) * row

                        while i < n
                            val c = src[i]

                            if c >= 128 then break

                            val t = tr[o + usize(c)]

                            if t < 0 then break

                            o = usize(t)
                            i += 1

                        s = int(o / row)
                else
                    var cur = Chars(src, i)
                    val c = cur.peek().unwrap()

                    cur.next()
                    s = self.step(usize(s), c)
                    i = cur.offset
        end find_end


Backward: where it begins
-------------------------

Knowing where the leftmost-longest match ends, where it begins is the earliest position from which
the pattern matches up to exactly there — anything earlier would have been the leftmost match
itself. The reversed program read right to left from the end, anchored there and taking the longest
match it can without passing where the search started, finds exactly that.

A character outside ASCII is found by stepping back to the byte that begins it. If decoding from
there does not land back where it started, the text is not well-formed UTF-8 and the answer is `-2`,
which sends the caller to the VM; a `string` never gives that.

        private[regex] find_start(*self, src: []const u8, from: usize, at: usize) -> int
            val n = src.len
            var s = self.start(at == 0, at == n)
            var i = at
            var last = -1

            loop
                val f = self.flags[usize(s)]

                if (f & has_accept) != 0 then last = int(i)

                if (f & is_dead) != 0 then return last

                if i <= from
                    if from == 0 && self.ends_here(s, true, n == 0) then last = 0

                    return last

                val b = src[i - 1]

                if b < 128
                    s = self.next(s, b)
                    i -= 1

                    if (self.flags[usize(s)] & (has_accept | is_dead)) == 0
                        val tr = self.trans.view()
                        var o = usize(s) * row

                        while i > from
                            val c = src[i - 1]

                            if c >= 128 then break

                            val t = tr[o + usize(c)]

                            if t < 0 then break

                            o = usize(t)
                            i -= 1

                        s = int(o / row)
                else
                    var k = i - 1

                    while k > from && !is_char_boundary(src[k])
                        k -= 1

                    var cur = Chars(src, k)
                    val c = cur.peek().unwrap()

                    cur.next()

                    if cur.offset != i then return -2

                    s = self.step(usize(s), c)
                    i = k
        end find_start
    end Dfa

    private is_accept(i: Inst) -> bool = i match
        Accept -> true
        else false

    private[regex] new_dfa(prog: Program, backward: bool) -> &Dfa
        var d: &Dfa = Dfa(prog, backward, buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), buf(), 0, 0)

        for _ in 0..<prog.len()
            d.marks.push(-1)

        for _ in 0..<4
            d.starts.push(-1)

        for _ in 0..<(cache_states * 2)
            d.table.push(0)

        d
    end new_dfa


What a search answers
---------------------

The match as the VM would report it, or that there is none, or that this engine cannot say and the
VM has to be asked — a starting position that is not on a character boundary, or text that is not
well-formed. The last case exists so that answering it is a decision made here, in the open, rather
than a guess.

    private[regex] enum Found
        Spans(slots: []int)
        Nothing
        AskTheVm
    end Found

    private[regex] dfa_find(prog: Program, fwd: &Dfa, rev: &Dfa, input: string, from: usize) -> Found
        val src = input.bytes

        if from > src.len || (from < src.len && !is_char_boundary(src[from])) then return AskTheVm

        val e = fwd.find_end(src, from, false)

        if e < 0 then return Nothing

        val b = rev.find_start(src, from, usize(e))

        if b < 0 then return AskTheVm

        if prog.groups == 0
            var slots: []int = [b, e]

            return Spans(slots)

        exec_span(prog, input, usize(b), usize(e)) match
            Some(slots) -> Spans(slots)
            None -> AskTheVm
    end dfa_find

The capture positions come from the VM, run from where the match begins and stopped where it ends.
Starting it there rather than at `from` gives the same groups: a thread the full run would have
started earlier never reaches an accept (the match would begin there otherwise), so the only thing
it could do in the full run is hold an instruction that a thread from the real start would then
find taken — and an instruction such a thread holds cannot lead to an accept either.
